  "title": "Conditional Knockout of LIF Receptor in Uterine Epithelium Disrupts Implantation",
  "abstract": "Targeted deletion of the LIF Receptor in the murine uterine epithelium leads to implantation failure. Leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF) is critical for preparing the uterus for blastocyst implantation. In mice, LIFR expression is primarily localized to the uterine luminal epithelium (LE). LIF, secreted from endometrial glands (GEs), binds to the LIFR, initiating the Janus kinase-signal transducer and activation of transcription (STAT) 3 (Jak-Stat3) signaling pathway in the LE. Activation of JAK-STAT converts the LE to a receptive state, enabling blastocysts to implant. To specifically eliminate LIFR in the LE, we generated mice with Cre recombinase inserted into the lactoferrin gene (Ltf-Cre). Lactoferrin expression in the LE is upregulated by E2, and Cre activity is restricted to the LE and GE. To assess LIFR's role in implantation, we created a conditional Lifr flx/flx mouse line. Crossing Ltf-Cre with Lifr flx/flx produced Lifr flx/D : Ltf Cre/+ females that appeared normal but were infertile. Many females failed to become pregnant despite repeated mating. Unimplanted blastocysts transferred to wild-type recipients implanted normally, indicating uterine receptivity was compromised. Loss of Lifr prevented STAT3 nuclear translocation and reduced Msx1 expression, a gene regulating uterine receptivity. These findings confirm that LIFR expression in the uterus is essential for implantation and identify key components of the LIF signaling pathway involved."
