  "abstract": "A novel point mutation in the helix 10 region of the human glucocorticoid receptor (GR) leads to generalized glucocorticoid resistance by altering the structure of the ligand-binding domain. This condition, characterized by partial insensitivity to glucocorticoids, is caused by mutations in the GR gene. In this case, a 2-year-old female patient presented with generalized seizures, hypoglycemia, and hypokalemia, along with hypertension and premature pubarche. Dexamethasone treatment effectively suppressed these symptoms. Genetic analysis revealed a heterozygous mutation (G-->A) at nucleotide position 2141 (exon 8) in the GR gene, resulting in the substitution of arginine by glutamine at amino acid position 714 in the ligand-binding domain. The mutant receptor showed impaired transactivation activity and reduced affinity for ligand. It also exhibited diminished binding to the p160 nuclear receptor coactivator. Structural analysis indicated that the arginine-to-glutamine substitution at position 714 caused conformational changes in the ligand-binding domain and the AF-2 transactivation surface, decreasing ligand and coactivator binding affinity. The mutation at position 714 plays a crucial role in maintaining the proper structure of the GR alpha ligand-binding domain and AF-2 surface."
