{
  "synthetic_id": "synthetic_twin_24709919_01",
  "source_doc_id": "24709919",
  "title": "Ginsenosides Modulate Opioid-Induced Hyperalgesia in Mice",
  "abstract": "Ginsenosides, including Re, Rg1, and Rb1 ginsenosides, were evaluated for their effects on opioid-induced hyperalgesia (OIH) in mice. OIH, a condition characterized by nociceptive sensitization following opioid withdrawal, can limit the clinical use of opioids and complicate recovery from opioid addiction. In this study, mice were administered morphine subcutaneously for 7 days, and on days 8 and 9, they received Re, Rg1, or Rb1 intragastrically. On day 10, thermal sensitivity and acetic acid-induced writhing tests were conducted. Re (300 mg/kg) inhibited OIH in both tests, while Rg1 and Rb1 failed to prevent OIH. Notably, Rg1 showed a tendency to exacerbate OIH in the acetic acid-induced writhing test. These findings suggest that Re, but not Rg1 or Rb1, may play a role in mitigating OIH."
