  "title": "SLURP1 Mutation and T-Cell Dysfunction in Mal de Meleda Patients",
  "abstract": "SLURP1 mutations have been linked to impaired T-cell activation in individuals with mal de Meleda. Mal de Meleda (MDM) is a rare autosomal recessive disorder characterized by palmoplantar erythrokeratoderma, caused by mutations in the SLURP1 gene. SLURP1 functions as an allosteric agonist for the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (nAchR), which is crucial for maintaining epidermal homeostasis. In addition, nAchR signaling plays a key role in regulating T-cell activity. Among patients with MDM, those carrying the G to A substitution in nucleotide 256 of the SLURP1 gene (resulting in a glycine to arginine substitution at amino acid 86, G86R) are at increased risk for melanoma and viral infection. These conditions may be associated with defective T-cell function and disrupted epidermal homeostasis. A study of a Taiwanese family with MDM revealed that individuals with the heterozygous or homozygous G86R mutation exhibited reduced T-cell activation. Treatment with recombinant human SLURP-1 protein restored normal T-cell function. These findings suggest that the presence of the G86R mutation in SLURP1 may contribute to T-cell dysfunction in MDM patients, highlighting the importance of SLURP1 in maintaining immune cell function."
