  "title": "Role of Adrenergic Receptors in Cocaine-Induced Anxiety",
  "abstract": "Norepinephrine signaling through beta-adrenergic receptors is essential for cocaine-induced anxiety. Cocaine-induced anxiety is a significant side effect of cocaine use, which is a commonly abused psychostimulant. While mechanisms behind cocaine's rewarding effects are well understood, the role of noradrenergic pathways in its aversive effects remains underexplored. In this study, dopamine beta-hydroxylase knockout (Dbh-/-) mice, which lack norepinephrine, were tested in the elevated plus maze (EPM) to assess the contribution of noradrenergic signaling to cocaine-induced anxiety. Cocaine dose-dependently increased anxiety-like behavior in control (Dbh+/-) mice, as evidenced by reduced open arm exploration. Dbh-/- mice showed normal baseline performance in the EPM but were resistant to cocaine's anxiogenic effects. Administration of disulfiram, a dopamine beta-hydroxylase inhibitor, reduced cocaine-induced anxiety in Dbh+/-. Using specific adrenergic antagonists, beta-adrenergic receptor antagonist propranolol blocked cocaine-induced anxiety in both Dbh+/- and wild-type mice, whereas alpha(1) antagonist prazosin and alpha(2) antagonist yohimbine had no effect. These findings highlight the critical role of beta-adrenergic receptors in mediating cocaine-induced anxiety."
