  "source_doc_id": "19445921",
  "title": "Antioxidant and Cardioprotective Effects of Salvianolic Acid A in Isoproterenol-Induced Myocardial Injury",
  "abstract": "This study investigates the cardioprotective effects of salvianolic acid A in isoproterenol-induced myocardial injury in rats. The experiment aimed to assess the protective potential of salvianolic acid A against isoproterenol-induced myocardial injury. Hemodynamic parameters and electrocardiogram recordings were continuously monitored. Serum and cardiac tissue levels of cardiac marker enzymes and antioxidant parameters were measured. Mitochondrial respiratory function assays and histopathological analysis of heart tissues were conducted. Rats treated with isoproterenol exhibited significant increases in lactate dehydrogenase, aspartate transaminase, creatine kinase, and malondialdehyde levels, along with decreased superoxide dismutase, catalase, and glutathione peroxidase activities in serum and heart tissues. These animals also showed reduced left ventricular systolic pressure, decreased maximum and minimum rate of pressure development, and elevated left ventricular end-diastolic pressure and ST-segment elevation. Mitochondrial respiratory dysfunction was observed, characterized by reduced respiratory control ratio and ADP/O. Administration of salvianolic acid A for 8 days significantly improved isoproterenol-induced cardiac dysfunction and myocardial injury, restoring mitochondrial respiratory function. Histopathological examination confirmed the protective effects of salvianolic acid A against isoproterenol-induced myocardial damage. The results indicate that salvianolic acid A, with its antioxidant properties, significantly protects against isoproterenol-induced myocardial injury."
