  "title": "Efficacy and safety of asenapine in a placebo- and haloperidol-controlled trial for schizophrenia",
  "abstract": "Asenapine is approved for the acute treatment of schizophrenia or manic episodes in bipolar I disorder. In a double-blind, 6-week trial, 458 patients with schizophrenia were randomly assigned to receive asenapine 5 mg twice daily, asenapine 10 mg twice daily, placebo, or haloperidol 4 mg twice daily. Using last observations carried forward, mean reductions in the Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale total score were significantly greater with asenapine 5 mg twice daily (-16.2) and haloperidol (-15.4) compared to placebo (-10.7; both P < 0.05). Mixed model for repeated measures showed greater changes at day 42 with asenapine 5 and 10 mg twice daily (-21.3 and -19.4) and haloperidol (-20.0) than placebo (-14.6; all P < 0.05). On the Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale positive subscale, all treatments were superior to placebo. Asenapine 5 mg twice daily was superior to placebo on the negative subscale with MMRM and on the general psychopathology subscale with LOCF and MMRM. Treatment-related adverse events occurred in 44% and 52% of asenapine 5 and 10 mg groups, 57% of haloperidol, and 41% of placebo. Extrapyramidal symptoms were reported in 15% and 18% of asenapine 5 and 10 mg groups, 34% of haloperidol, and 10% of placebo. No more than 5% of patients had clinically significant weight change. Post hoc analyses showed similar efficacy with asenapine and haloperidol, with greater contrasts in adverse events, especially extrapyramidal symptoms."
